# Every layer with parts in its own document, which is the `childLaneSet` population. One row per
# composed layer that carries at least one local part.
#
# BPMN already has the element
#   `Lane` carries an optional `childLaneSet` for lanes within a lane, and "a fusion's parts divide
#   what they compose" is that sentence. A `subProcess` would say a local fusion is an activity
#   inside a lane; it divides one, and the difference is that a subProcess leaves layer grain while
#   a nested lane does not.
#
# The grain is the whole point
#   A `callActivity` names a process, so a foreign part collapses from layer to layer into filing to
#   filing, and the emitted graph can carry a loop the model does not have. A nested lane is still a
#   lane, keyed `(filing, layer)`, so nothing collapses and nothing can be invented. Every part this
#   relation names is a part removed from that exposure.
#
# One row per parent, not per part
#   A `childLaneSet` is one element however many lanes it holds. The child lanes are counted by the
#   `lanes` law, which is unchanged: every layer is still exactly one lane, and nesting moves it
#   rather than duplicating it.
#
# A parent lane holds no `flowNodeRef`s
#   A lane holding a `childLaneSet` must not also hold `flowNodeRef`s, and that is checked rather
#   than assumed: on the corpus no local fusion carries a draw or a foreign part, so no parent lane
#   owes both. The day one does, the emission owes a decision, and this header is where the reader
#   is told there is one.
-- diagrams/calls.sqlc pinned to local parts, folded to the parent lane that will hold them.
SELECT DISTINCT c.composition, c.composed_layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.is_local
